Our prototype directly addresses the core operational problem in routine gas chromatography (GC2) quality control: analysts either spend excessive time manually reviewing routine, passing runs or miss subtle, early-stage instrument drift—while introducing generative AI directly into lab operations risks false alarms, hallucinated root causes, and compliance violations.

By intentionally scoping the prototype to one instrument (GC2), one SPC workflow, and one conditional AI-review path, we solve this problem through a focused, two-step architecture:

Eliminating Alert Fatigue & Missed Drift (Rule-Based Power BI Screening): Instead of relying on manual weekly chart reviews or sending every reading to an AI, deterministic math rules in Power BI automatically screen every run against fixed lab-standard limits. Routine runs pass silently with zero alerts, while genuine Out-of-Control (OOC) spikes and subtle six-point trends (which manual reviews frequently miss before a limit breach) are immediately flagged. Invalid or malformed records are isolated via a separate data-quality flag so broken feeds never trigger false process investigations.

Accelerating Root-Cause Investigation Without Adding Manual Lookup Work (Power Automate Context Bundling): When an anomaly occurs, technicians normally have to manually cross-reference chromatograms against historical baselines and daily room logs. Our user-triggered Power Automate flow automatically packages the flagged run, the prior 10 valid runs, and the surrounding 3 days of environmental telemetry (DailyLogs.xlsx) into a single structured payload—cutting investigation time while preserving strict data isolation.

Preventing AI Hallucinations & Protecting Compliance (Guardrailed Advisory Output): Rather than letting AI make autonomous pass/fail calls or invent missing lab context, the prompt restricts the AI to strictly advisory explanations grounded solely in the supplied JSON payload. It highlights supported observations, explicitly flags missing environmental logs (as tested in TC05), and recommends concrete technician checks—leaving all physical verification, batch holds, and final dispositions in AI_Review_Log.xlsx firmly under human and lab-manager authority.
